.TH FSTAT 8 "December 12, 1987"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
fstat \- file status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fstat
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-u
user ] [
.B \-p
pid ] [
.B filename...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fstat
identifies open files.
A file is considered open if a process has it open
or if it is the working directory for a process.
If no options are specified,
.I fstat
reports on all open files of the processes it may inspect.
.PP
.I Fstat
reads the kernel's process and descriptor records, which
.IR sysctl (3)
returns for
.B KERN_PROC
and
.BR KERN_FILE .
The kernel reads a process's descriptor table wherever its image lives \-
in memory, on the swap device or in the SwapRAM pool \- so
.I fstat
reads no memory device and needs no privilege.
The kernel returns the descriptors of a process to the super-user and to a
caller whose effective user ID is the process's real or effective user ID;
the other processes are left out.
.PP
Options:
.TP 6
.B  \-v
Name each process left out and the reason.
.TP 6
.B  \-u
Report all files open by a specified user.
.TP 6
.B  \-p
Report all files open by a specified process id.
.TP 6
.B filename...
Restrict reports to the specified files.
If the file is a block special file,
.I fstat
additionally reports on any open files on that device,
treating it as a mounted file system.
.PP
The following fields are printed
.TP 6
.B  USER
The username of the owner of the process.
.TP 6
.B CMD
The command name of the process.
.TP 6
.B PID
The process id.
.TP 6
.B FD
The file number in the per-process open file table.  The special
name "wd" means that the file is the working directory for the process.
If the file number is followed by an asterisk (*), then the file is not
an inode, and the rest of the entry names its type in parentheses.
.TP 6
.B DEVICE
Major minor number of the device this file exists on.
.TP 6
.B INODE
The inode number of the file.
.TP 6
.B SIZE
The size in bytes of the file.
.TP 6
.B TYPE
The type of the file: reg, dir, chr, blk, lnk or soc from its inode mode,
or pip for a pipe.
.SH BUGS
Since \fIfstat\fP takes a snapshot of the system, it is only correct for
a very short period of time.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ps(1), pstat(8), sysctl(3)
